{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q005_cupredoxin_function_transfer","answer":"SCOPe places these proteins in distinct families within the Cupredoxins superfamily (b.6.1), under the cupredoxin-like fold (b.6) in the all-β class (b). These include the Plastocyanin/azurin-like family (b.6.1.1), ephrins, and the N-terminal domain of peptidylarginine deiminase PAD4. Thus, SCOPe recognizes a remote evolutionary relationship at the superfamily level, stronger than merely sharing a fold, while distinguishing their families and biological specializations.

Copper binding and electron transfer should not be transferred across these families. Plastocyanin and azurin are canonical copper-dependent electron carriers. Ephrin ectodomains bind Eph receptors in cell signaling; their cupredoxin ancestry does not establish a copper-binding or electron-transfer role. PAD4 catalyzes calcium-dependent protein arginine deimination, with its catalytic machinery in the C-terminal domain; classification of its N-terminal domain as a cupredoxin relative does not make that domain a copper-dependent electron carrier.

A shared structural framework can guide domain-level structural comparison or template selection, but does not establish conserved metal ligands, binding loops, redox properties, or whole-protein function. Even within a family, copper binding requires scrutiny of the ligand constellation and its three-dimensional geometry. Strong functional assignments require independent evidence such as metal occupancy and coordination in structures, metal-binding or spectroscopic measurements, and biochemical electron-transfer assays. Neither homology nor copper binding alone proves physiological electron-transfer function.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
